I think that using trigo you can make the answer much more precise and short and I have one simple and short answer here.

The answer goes something like this:

Using Midpoint Theorem on Points  $F$  and  $D$  we have  $FD \parallel AC$ .
which makes  $\angle A= \angle BFD$ Applying Circumradius  $R= \frac a{2 \sin A}$  we have
\begin{align*}
R &=\frac{2 \cdot BD}{2 \cdot \sin \angle BFD} 
    &=\frac{BD}{ \sin \angle BFD} 
   & = \frac{BD}{ \sin \angle BND}   \ \ \ \ \ \ (BNFD \text{ cyclic}) 
   & = \frac{ND}{ \sin \angle NBD}    \ \ \ \ \ \ (\text{sin rule in } \triangle BND) 
   & = \frac{ND}{ \sin 90^{\circ}} 
   & = \boxed{ND}
\end{align*}

Drop a perpendicular from  $I$  to  $AC$  and let the touch-point with  $AC$  be  $G$ , it is easy to check  $\angle AEF=\angle AIF=\angle ABC=90^{\circ}$ , now we length chase as we have  $r^2=FG\cdot AG$ , we have
\begin{align*}
AG&=\frac{AB-BC+CA}{2} FG&=\frac{AC}{2}-\left(\frac{AB-BC+CA}{2}\right)=\frac{BC-AB}{2}
\implies r^2&=\frac{(AB-BC+CA)(BC-AB)}{4}
\end{align*}
and  $r=\frac{AB+BC-CA}{2}$  so we have   $$ (AB+BC-CA)^2=(BC-AB)(AC+AB-BC)\iff 3BC+AB-3AC=0 $$   substitute PGT, then do squaring to get  $\boxed{\frac{BC}{AB}=\frac{4}{3}}$ .

