Let  $I$  be the incenter of  $\triangle ABC$ , and let the circumradius of  $\triangle ABC$  be  $R$ . Note that the circumradius of  $\triangle BHC$  is also  $R$ : To see this, one may use sin rule \[R_{BHC} = \dfrac{BC}{2\sin \angle BHC} = \dfrac{BC}{2\sin(180^\circ - \angle BAC)}= \dfrac{BC}{2\sin \angle BAC} = R, \] or else one may note that the circumcircle of  $\triangle BHC$  is the reflection of the circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$  about  $BC$  (since the reflection of  $H$  across  $BC$  lies on  $\Omega$ ).

  $M$  is the unique point satisfying  $MH = R$ ,  $MB=MC$  and  $M$  lies on opposite side of  $BC$  as  $H$  (this is because  $H$  lies in the interior of the triangle whereas  $M$  lies in the exterior), so it must be the circumcenter of  $\triangle BHC$ . 

 Now  $M$  is also the circumcenter of  $\triangle BIC$  by the Incenter-Excenter lemma, so we have  $B,H,I,C$  cyclic with center  $M$ . Thus,   $180^\circ - \angle BAC = \angle BHC =\angle BIC = 90^\circ + \angle BAC /2$ , giving that  $\angle BAC = 60^\circ$  as needed.  $\blacksquare$</details>

