Our answer is  $\boxed{\frac{\sqrt 6}{3}}$ . Notice  $(1,1,0)$  gives us
\[K+1 \ge (K+1) \cdot \sqrt{\frac 23}.\]

We claim this is sufficent for all  $a,b,c \in [0,1]$ . Manipulating for  $K = \frac{\sqrt 6}{3}$ , we get
\begin{align*}
&\hspace{2cm} \frac{\sqrt 6}{3} + \frac{a+b+c}{3} \ge \left(\frac{\sqrt 6}{3} + 1\right) \sqrt{\frac{a^2+b^2+c^2}{3}} 
&\iff 6 + 2\sqrt 6 (a+b+c) + (a+b+c)^2 \ge (5+2\sqrt 6)(a^2+b^2+c^2) 
&\iff 3 + \sqrt 6 (a+b+c) + (ab+bc+ca) \ge (2+\sqrt 6)(a^2+b^2+c^2)
\end{align*}

We conclude by noting
\begin{align*}
3 \ge 2&a^2 + b^2 
a \sqrt 6  \ge a^2 \sqrt 6, \quad b \sqrt 6  &\ge b^2 \sqrt 6, \quad c \sqrt 6  \ge c^2 \sqrt 6 
ab \ge b^2, \quad bc &\ge c^2, \quad ca \ge c^2.~\blacksquare
\end{align*}

