Observe that a positive integer  $n$  is asri if and only if we may express
\[n+500 = (a+25)(b+20)\]
That is,  $n+500$  is a composite integer which can be decomposed into two factors such that one is greater than 20 and then other is greater than 25.

(a) Consider any prime  $P>20^{25}+500$  which must exist due to the infinitude of the primes. Hence,  $P-500$  is not asri since if it were,
\[P=(a+25)(b+20)\]
contradicting our assumption that  $P$  was prime.

(b) Let  $n=m-500$  where  $m$  is (a sufficiently large) positive integer which satisfies the conditions, 
