m & \equiv -21 \pmod{47}
\end{align*}
which exists by the Chinese Remainder Theorem. It is not hard to check (simply write out the factors using the above criteria) that  $26 \mid m , 27 \mid m+1, \dots , 51 \mid m+25$  for this choice of  $m$ . Moreover, since  $m$  is sufficiently large the other factor will also exceed  $20$  implying that  $n$  must be asri, as desired.

